  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best approach is to use the CLI tools (either the <strong>EB CLI</strong> or <strong>AWS CLI</strong>) to update your Elastic Beanstalk environment configuration, for instance through <code>aws elasticbeanstalk update-environment</code> or similar commands. This lets you apply your saved configuration as a base, and then add or modify specific settings for your performance test pipelines <strong>without altering the original YAML file</strong>.</p><p>Let's break down why the other options aren't as effective:</p><ul><li><i>\"Modify the standard settings for the performance testing environment directly through the Elastic Beanstalk console, and then apply the saved configuration for new pipelines.\"</i> – If you apply the saved configuration after console changes, your modifications might be overwritten, and this doesn't address how to apply only specific changes without editing the base config file.</li><li><i>\"Prepare a file with the updated configuration options and place it in the <code>.ebextensions</code> directory, making sure this folder resides within the application root.\"</i> – While <code>.ebextensions</code> allow you to specify environment settings, you must deploy a new version of your application to take effect, which may not suit cases where you want to adjust settings post-deployment or across multiple environments quickly.</li><li><i>\"Recreate the saved configuration and edit it with EB CLI commands, such as <code>eb config save --cfg newcfg</code>.\"</i> – This requires manual recomposition or edit of the configuration file, which the scenario specifically wants to avoid.</li></ul><p><strong>Tip:</strong> The CLI methods facilitate targeted updates and environment flexibility without manual YAML file manipulation, streamlining your deployment automation or pipeline integration.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have an application running on AWS Elastic Beanstalk. You previously saved a configuration from your test environment. Now, you wish to set up new pipelines for a performance testing environment using this saved configuration. However, you notice some environment settings require changes for your new performance environment. You prefer not to alter the original saved configuration YAML file. Which course of action should you take to accomplish this?</p>",
  "original_oid": "6793d9c761969b416038a0ae",
